/* Dubai Building Code (2021 Edition) – Part G: Incoming utilities (DEWA requirements for electrical installations).
 * Transcribed from the page images of the supplied PDF. pg = printed page ("G 18"); f = PDF page (image pages/dbc/p-NN.jpg). Verify against the original before use. */
(D.real = D.real || []).push(
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.2', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.2', cl: 'G.4.1–G.4.2', pg: 'G 16', f: 9, t: 'Electrical supply from DEWA', s: ['MDB', 'Short Circuit'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Electrical'],
  x: 'Sections G.4 to G.7 outline the requirements for the design of electrical installations. They are based on the latest edition of BS 7671 and Institution of Engineering Technology (IET) documents. The nominal electric supply voltage from DEWA (see IEC 60038) is 230/400 V ± 10%, 50 Hz, three-phase, 4-wire with separate neutral and protective conductor (generally metallic armour of the DEWA service cable). The neutral is solidly earthed at DEWA’s substations and shall not be earthed elsewhere in the consumer’s electrical installations. The design fault level within the substation is 40 kA (fault duration 1 s), except for fuse protected equipment/circuits. Overload, short-circuit and earth leakage protective devices shall be provided.', a: { supply: '230/400 V ±10%, 50 Hz, 3-ph 4-wire; design fault level 40 kA (1 s)' } },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.3', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.3', cl: 'G.4.3', pg: 'G 16', f: 9, t: 'Incoming supply and metering – substation threshold and submeters', s: ['Transformer', 'Load Estimation'], k: ['Transformer', 'Electrical'],
  x: 'Before commencing building design, the consumer shall obtain confirmation of the availability of a power supply from DEWA. Where the total connected load (TCL) exceeds 400 kW, provision shall be made within the building or plot for a DEWA substation. NOTE 2: In some circumstances a DEWA substation might be required for connected loads less than 400 kW. Meters shall be installed to measure and record the electricity demand and consumption of the facility. All meters shall conform to DEWA specifications and be approved by DEWA. When a building tariff meter is not present, sub meters shall be installed for each individual tenancy in the building. Virtual meters using run-hours shall not be used as submeters.', a: { substation_threshold: '400 kW connected load' } },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.4.1', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.4.1', cl: 'G.4.4.1', pg: 'G 17', f: 10, t: 'Point of supply – main circuit breaker and incoming terminations', s: ['MDB', 'Circuit Breaker'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Electrical'],
  x: 'Point of supply shall be made available at one location within a plot/project, unless otherwise approved by DEWA. The point of supply defines the boundary of DEWA equipment, and shall be decided by DEWA. The main circuit breaker at the point of supply shall be marked as such and shall be identifiable from other breakers for easy operation in an emergency. Where a main low voltage (LV) distribution panel is connected directly to the LV side of the transformer, the main incomer circuit breaker proposed in the LV panel shall be a fully withdrawable four-pole type air circuit breaker (ACB). Where the consumer’s main LV distribution board (DB)/panel is connected to DEWA’s two or more distribution transformers, separate bus-sections with mechanically and electrically interlocked bus-section breakers/isolators (four-pole) shall be provided. All incoming cable terminations/live connections in metering cabinets, MDBs and sub-main distribution boards (SMDBs) shall be shrouded and insulated. All exposed live terminal connections and busbars in any LV DB shall be shrouded and/or insulated.' },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.4.2', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.4.2', cl: 'G.4.4.2', pg: 'G 18', f: 11, t: 'Switchgear locations – main switchroom, clearances, ventilation and emergency lighting', s: ['DB', 'MDB', 'SMDB', 'Emergency Lighting'], k: ['Electrical', 'Low Voltage', 'Lighting'], fig: 'dbclear',
  x: 'The main electrical switchroom shall be located near to the plot entrance, on the ground floor. Where a substation is provided within the building, the main electrical switchroom shall be provided in the ground floor area adjacent to the transformer room. The consumer’s MDBs, SMDBs and final DBs shall all be installed in locations to which access is always available. The minimum dimensions for safe access shall be in accordance with Figure G.3. The mounting height [to the top of the board(s)] shall normally be 2 m from the ground/floor level. All main electrical switchrooms, and other sub-electrical switchrooms in which capacitor banks are installed, shall be air-conditioned. Non-air-conditioned electrical rooms shall be ventilated and provided with heavy duty exhaust fans and metallic louvered doors, as applicable. As required by Table 1.9, Ch. 1 of UAE FLSC, interior electrical rooms shall be separated from the rest of the building by a 2 h fire rated enclosure. If the access door to the room opens to the exterior then it can be louvered. Where the access door opens to the interior of the building it shall be 90 min fire rated without louvers. An adequate level of illumination shall be provided to facilitate safe operation at all times. All electrical rooms shall be provided with non-maintained emergency lighting in accordance with Ch. 6 of UAE FLSC (Figure G.3 key: non-maintained, minimum 3 h rated, self-contained emergency light). Door openings to electrical switchrooms shall: a) open outwards in the direction of egress; b) be kept free from obstructions; c) not open towards a driveway, staircases and steps, or other areas of people or traffic movement. Figure G.3 minimum clear space: 1,500 mm in front of the panel; 750 mm at the sides and rear (rear only for switchboards with rear access).',
  a: { front_clear: '1500 mm (front); 750 mm sides/rear', db_height: '≤ 2000 mm to top of DB', duration: '180 min', location: 'All electrical rooms (non-maintained, self-contained)', el_standard: 'Ch. 6 UAE FLSC' },
  e: 'Panel front clearance is 1,500 mm with 750 mm at the sides (and 750 mm at the rear for rear-access boards). Every electrical room needs non-maintained self-contained emergency lighting rated for at least 3 hours, and the door opens outwards in the direction of egress.', rec: 'Draw the clearance zones from Figure G.3 on the switchroom layout, add the 3 h emergency luminaire, and coordinate the 2 h fire-rated enclosure with the architect.' },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.4.3', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.4.3', cl: 'G.4.4.3', pg: 'G 19', f: 12, t: 'Main electrical switchroom – illumination, wet areas and layout', s: ['MDB', 'DB', 'Lux Requirements'], k: ['Electrical', 'Low Voltage', 'Lighting'],
  x: 'A minimum illumination of 500 lux shall be provided to facilitate safe operation at all times. Incoming supply cables to the consumer’s MDB(s) shall be completely segregated and identified from the consumer’s cables. Electrical switchrooms shall not be located below or beside the following wet areas: 1) bathrooms; 2) toilets; 3) kitchens; 4) pantries; 5) storage tanks; 6) air conditioning chillers or other liquid/hazardous materials. Water pipes shall not be installed within or on electrical switchroom walls. The dimensional layout of electrical switchrooms shall be maintained as shown in Figure G.3 for a single LV panel, Figure G.4 for switchrooms containing two freestanding LV panels or Figure G.5 for switchrooms containing wall mounted LV panels. The electrical switchroom shall not be used for storage of any equipment, material, etc.', a: { switchroom_lux: '500 lux' } },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.5', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.5', cl: 'G.4.5.1–G.4.5.3', pg: 'G 22', f: 14, t: 'Tariff metering – multiple consumer premises and metering cabinet space', s: ['MDB', 'Load Estimation'], k: ['Electrical', 'Low Voltage'],
  x: 'A minimum clearance of 2 m shall be maintained between electricity and water service cabinets/points. For multiple consumer premises (residential/commercial buildings, industries, large utility complexes and schools) the MDBs and SMDBs, with associated metering, shall be installed in separate electrical switchrooms located close to the entrance boundary line. For all buildings having a cooling load of at least 1 MW, or a gross floor area of 1,000 m² or greater, additional electrical submetering (of tariff class accuracy) shall be installed. The submetering shall record demand and consumption data for each energy-consuming system in the building with a load of 100 kW or greater. All tariff metering shall be smart meters, normally provided by DEWA and restricted to one for each consumer installation. The minimum space provided for installation of a kWh meter shall be 300 mm wide and 500 mm high. A minimum space of 1,200 mm shall be provided in the front of kWh meter cabinet/meters.', a: { meter_front_space: '1200 mm', submeter_threshold: 'Cooling load ≥ 1 MW or GFA ≥ 1,000 m²; systems ≥ 100 kW' } },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.5.4', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.5.4', cl: 'G.4.5.4', pg: 'G 24', f: 15, t: 'CT metering requirements', s: ['MDB', 'Circuit Breaker'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Electrical'], x: 'Metering by means of current transformers (CTs) shall be installed where the circuit breaker rating at the point of supply is 160 A and above. DEWA provides the smart kWh meter(s) and associated CTs for all tariff metering. The CTs shall be located on the busbars immediately after the circuit breaker/isolator at which the complete installation is to be metered. Removable links of adequate length shall be provided in the busbar of each phase to enable easy maintenance and replacement of CTs. Three CTs shall be provided for each metering. The following CT transformation ratios shall be used: 200/5; 300/5; 400/5; 800/5; 1,600/5; 2,400/5.', a: { ct_threshold: 'CT metering at 160 A and above' } },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.6', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.6', cl: 'G.4.6 / Table G.3', pg: 'G 26', f: 16, t: 'Ambient design conditions for Dubai', s: ['Cable Sizing'], k: ['Electrical', 'Low Voltage'], x: 'All equipment, apparatus, materials and accessories used in electrical installations shall be suitable for the purpose intended and capable of operating with satisfactory performance in the climatic conditions described in Table G.3: altitude – sea level (coastal); maximum outdoor ambient temperature (shade) 48 °C; minimum ambient air temperature 2.8 °C; maximum ambient air temperature 48 °C; maximum average temperature over 24 h 37.8 °C; maximum average over one year 26.9 °C; relative humidity 100% (max.); thunderstorms – occasional; earthquake loading 0.07 g; wind speed 45 m/s at 10 m height; ground temperature 40 °C; soil thermal resistivity 2.0 °C·m/W at depth of 0.9 m. NOTE: Heavy condensation and sandstorms also prevail.', a: { ambient: '48 °C max ambient; ground 40 °C; soil 2.0 °C·m/W at 0.9 m' } },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.7.1', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.7', cl: 'G.4.7.1–G.4.7.2', pg: 'G 26', f: 16, t: 'Cables and conductors – selection and minimum conductor size', s: ['Cable', 'Cable Sizing', 'Socket Outlets'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Electrical', 'Power'],
  x: 'For general purposes and in normal situations, LSF/XLPE insulated, stranded copper conductor cables shall be used for all fixed wiring installations. The cables shall be appropriate to the building type and conform to the respective standard from the following list: BS 5467; BS 6004; BS 6724; BS 7211; BS 7629-1; BS 7846; BS 7889; BS 8436. In certain circumstances, PVC insulated cabling may be used for general purpose installations, subject to approval by DEWA. In flammable/explosive situations, cables shall be selected in accordance with the requirements of NFPA 70, as required by Ch. 10 of UAE FLSC. Flexible cables and cords for use in electrical installations shall be LSF insulated and sheathed, stranded copper conductors conforming to BS 7211. Single core cables armoured with steel wire or tape shall not be used for AC circuits. The size of conductor used for lighting circuits shall be not less than 2.5 mm². The size of conductor used for utility sockets shall be not less than 4 mm².', a: { conductor_light: '2.5 mm²', socket_conductor: '4 mm²' } },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.7.3', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.7', cl: 'G.4.7.3', pg: 'G 27', f: 17, t: 'Cable current rating, size and voltage drop', s: ['Voltage Drop', 'Cable', 'Cable Sizing', 'Circuit Breaker'], k: ['Electrical', 'Low Voltage', 'Power'],
  x: 'All cables shall be sized to continuously carry the normal current of the individual circuits based on various laying conditions as applicable and the maximum ambient temperature (see BS 7769). The cable sizes for general purpose applications should typically be not less than those given in Table G.4, Table G.5 and Table G.6. The voltage drop from the point of supply to any point in the wiring installations shall be not more than 4% of the nominal voltage of the electric supply, unless otherwise specified. Typical recommended cable sizes – Table G.6 (multicore armoured XLPE insulated cables, copper): 10 mm² – 50 A; 16 mm² – 60 A; 25 mm² – 80 A; 35 mm² – 100 A; 50 mm² – 125 A; 70 mm² – 160 A; 95 mm² – 200 A; 120 mm² – 225 A; 150 mm² – 250 A; 185 mm² – 300 A; 240 mm² – 350 A; 300 mm² – 400 A (max. rating of MCB/MCCB and max. load current). Table G.4 (single core LSF in concealed conduits): 2.5 mm² – 10/15 A; 4 mm² – 20 A; 6 mm² – 25 A; 10 mm² – 40 A; 16 mm² – 50 A.',
  a: { vd_light: '4%', vd_power: '4%' },
  e: 'The 4% voltage-drop limit applies from the point of supply to any point in the wiring installation, so feeder, sub-main and final circuit drops add together. Tables G.4–G.6 give the minimum typical cable sizes for each breaker rating.', rec: 'Keep a voltage-drop schedule per distribution path and check the sum is ≤ 4%; start cable selection from Tables G.4–G.6 and then apply derating for the 48 °C ambient.' },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.7.4', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.7–G.4.11', cl: 'G.4.7.4–G.4.11.1', pg: 'G 28', f: 18, t: 'Cable colours, high temperatures, hazardous areas, load balancing and conduits', s: ['Cable'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Electrical'], x: 'Colour identification of non-flexible cables: earth continuity conductor green and yellow; neutral black; phase conductors red, yellow, blue (R-phase red, Y-phase yellow, B-phase blue). Flexible cables: live brown, neutral blue, earth green and yellow. For end connections to luminaires with incandescent and halogen lamps, and fixed heating appliances, heat-resistant sleeves and cables shall be rated for a minimum operating temperature of 85 °C. Electrical equipment in potentially hazardous atmospheres shall be selected in accordance with BS EN 60079. In all cases where a three-phase supply is provided, the various categories of connected load shall be distributed and connected on red, yellow and blue phases as evenly as possible. High impact rigid PVC conduits shall conform to BS 4607, BS EN 60423 and BS EN 61386, be suitable for use at a maximum ambient temperature of 48 °C, not soften or suffer structural degradation at 70 °C, be non-hygroscopic and heat-resistant. Steel conduits shall be hot-dip galvanized to Class 4 protection, both inside and outside.', a: { phase_colours: 'Red / Yellow / Blue; neutral black' } },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.11.2', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.11–G.4.12', cl: 'G.4.11.2–G.4.12.1', pg: 'G 29', f: 19, t: 'Trunking, cable trays and MDB/SMDB construction', s: ['Cable', 'MDB', 'SMDB'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Electrical'], x: 'Trunking and fittings shall conform to BS EN 50085 and be constructed of steel, hot-dip galvanized both inside and outside, or non-combustible insulating material with removable covers. The protective conductor shall run inside the trunking and not in parallel. Small insulated cables shall not be installed in perforated trunking or perforated cable trays. Cable trays shall be either hot-dip galvanized or PVC-coated steel, perforated or ladder type, with upstands, and conform to BS EN 61537. MDBs and SMDBs installed within the consumer installations shall be factory-built assemblies conforming to BS EN 61439/IEC 61439. They shall be constructed of materials capable of withstanding mechanical, electrical and thermal stress and the effects of humidity. The phase busbar, neutral bar and earth bar shall be copper. The neutral bar shall be of the same cross-section as the phase busbar.' },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.12', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.12', cl: 'G.4.12.1–G.4.12.2', pg: 'G 32', f: 20, t: 'MDB/SMDB instruments and final distribution boards', s: ['MDB', 'SMDB', 'DB', 'Circuit Breaker'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Electrical'], x: 'The following shall be provided in MDBs with a rating of 200 A or more, and may be provided in SMDBs if appropriate: a) voltmeter (with R-Y-B “OFF” selector switch); b) ammeter (with CTs as applicable); c) maximum demand indicator/recorder; d) PF meter; e) indicating lamps; f) associated protective devices. The switchgear, equipment and accessories shall conform to: BS EN 60670; BS EN 60898-1; BS EN 60947; BS EN 61439; IEC 61439. DBs installed for connection of the final circuits shall be factory-built conforming to BS EN 61439/IEC 61439. An integral isolator shall be provided for isolation of the incoming supply. Rewireable type fuses shall not be used in any wiring installation. Dielectric test of DBs/MDBs: 2,500/2,125 V (85%).', a: { mdb_metering: 'V, A, MD, PF meter on MDBs ≥ 200 A' } },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.13.1', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.13', cl: 'G.4.13.1', pg: 'G 35', f: 21, t: 'Switches', s: ['Lighting Controls'], k: ['Lighting', 'Electrical'], x: 'Switches provided for local isolation of electric supply to individual apparatus and/or circuits shall conform to BS EN 60669. The current rating shall be not less than 5 A. Switches shall be metal clad when used within industrial installations. Switches installed for control of discharge lighting shall have a minimum current rating that is twice the steady state continuous current of the circuits. For large groups of lighting, a gang switch with phase barrier inside the switch boxes shall be provided. For outdoor locations, switches with weather protection classified as IP55 in accordance with BS EN 60529 shall be used. For areas with a high risk of fire or explosion, gas sealed switches conforming to BS EN 60079 shall be used. For appliances rated 20 A and above, a DP switch with neon indicator shall be provided.' },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.13.2', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.13', cl: 'G.4.13.2–G.4.13.3', pg: 'G 36', f: 22, t: 'Plugs and socket outlets; arc fault detection devices (AFDDs)', s: ['Socket Outlets', 'Small Power', 'Protection'], k: ['Power', 'Low Voltage'], x: 'Single-phase plugs and socket outlets used in domestic and commercial installations shall conform to BS 1363. The socket outlets shall be shuttered, double-pole, 3-pin flat with switch. 15 A plugs and socket outlets used in domestic and commercial installations shall conform to BS 546. Shaver socket outlets shall conform to BS EN 61558 in bathrooms and BS 4573 in rooms other than bathrooms. Industrial plugs and socket outlets shall conform to BS EN 60309 and shall feature a switch that is integrally built in or attached to them. Weather protected type (IP55) socket outlets shall be used for outdoor applications. Additional protection to mitigate the risk of fire due to arcing is recommended in certain applications (premises with sleeping accommodation, risk of fire due to processed or stored materials, combustible construction materials, structures with fire propagating features, endangered or irreplaceable objects). AFDDs shall be selected in accordance with BS EN 62606 and placed at the origin of the circuit to be protected.' },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.13.4', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.13', cl: 'G.4.13.4–G.4.13.5', pg: 'G 37', f: 23, t: 'Surge protection devices and cooker control units', s: ['Protection', 'Socket Outlets', 'RCD', 'Lightning Protection'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Power', 'Lightning Protection'], x: 'Surge protection devices shall be implemented on electrical distribution systems within new buildings. SPDs shall have the necessary capability to deal with the current levels and durations involved at the point of installation. For the protection of AC power circuits, SPDs are allocated a type number: type 1 – only used where there is a risk of direct lightning current and typically used at the origin of an installation; type 2 – used at DBs; type 3 – used at or near equipment. All stationary cooking appliances in domestic premises shall have a cooker control switch conforming to BS 4177 located separately from the appliance, within 2 m of the appliance. The cooker control switch shall be two-pole (single-phase appliance) or four-pole (three-phase appliance) and connected to a separate final sub-circuit from the DB through a 100 mA ELCB.', a: { spd: 'SPD mandatory in new buildings (Type 1 at origin, Type 2 at DBs, Type 3 at equipment)' } },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.13.7', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.13', cl: 'G.4.13.7–G.4.13.11', pg: 'G 39', f: 25, t: 'Water heaters, air-conditioning, extra-LV apparatus, HV discharge lighting and motors', s: ['Motors', 'HVAC', 'Protection', 'RCD'], k: ['Power', 'Low Voltage'], x: 'Water heaters, saunas, Jacuzzis and washing machines shall be connected to a separate final sub-circuit from the DB; every heater circuit shall be protected by a 30 mA RCCB/ELCB. Window-type room air conditioners with a maximum cooling capacity of 1.5 kW shall be connected by a 15 A switched socket outlet. Each room air-conditioning unit shall be connected to a separate final sub-circuit from the DB. A maximum of two window-type air-conditioning units are permitted to connect on a single-phase supply. The breaker rating and wire size shall be selected based on the connected load of the air conditioning unit, subject to minimum 20 A with 4 mm² circuit wires. HV discharge lighting equipment shall conform to BS 559 and be rated for voltages not exceeding 5 kV RMS to earth. Every motor having a rating exceeding 0.37 kW shall be provided with control equipment incorporating means of protection against overload. Only the installation of single-phase motors rated up to 3.7 kW, and three-phase motors up to 110 kW, is permitted, unless otherwise approved by DEWA.', a: { max_motor_3ph: '110 kW three-phase; 3.7 kW single-phase' } },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.13.11', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.13', cl: 'G.4.13.11 (cont.)', pg: 'G 40', f: 26, t: 'Motor starters, isolators and labelling', s: ['Motors'], k: ['Power', 'Low Voltage'], x: 'Where multiple motors above 110 kW are proposed, the advice of DEWA shall be sought on obtaining a bulk supply. Starters shall be provided with overload relays of the thermal type with automatic compensation for variations in ambient temperature between 28 °C and 48 °C. Starting equipment to limit current shall consist of any of: adjustable speed drive; intelligent controllers; another type of device approved by DEWA. All motors shall be provided with an isolator, interrupting the supply on all phases, in close proximity to the motor. The control gear shall incorporate emergency stop pushbutton(s). All starters, isolators and pushbuttons shall be clearly marked in Arabic and English stating which machine they control and their function; the words “START” and “STOP” shall be used (and not “OPEN” and “CLOSED”).' },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.14', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.14', cl: 'G.4.14', pg: 'G 40–41', f: 27, t: 'Standby generators', s: ['Generator'], k: ['Generator', 'Electrical'], fig: 'genroom',
  x: 'Standby safety power provision shall be made for all projects and premises in the category of touristic, commercial and cultural, that require dedicated transformer and LV panel supplies, including shopping malls and centres, hotel buildings, amusement and theme parks, museums, major exhibition halls, hospitals and main government buildings. For new and existing buildings the following are considered essential services/loads: a separate main and/or sub-main LV panel; in-house standby generator(s) of sufficient capacity for 100% backup, with auto-changeover functionality (ATS). The following loads shall be served by standby generators: 1) elevators, escalators, security systems, CCTV, alarm systems, BMS systems, stairways, entrances, control rooms, data centres, electrical rooms, generator rooms, etc.; 2) minimum 20% of lighting installations in the landlord areas (corridors, public lobbies, assembly points, reception, underground parking, etc.). Generator rooms shall be 2 h fire rated to conform to Table 1.9, Ch. 1 of UAE FLSC. Installing and connecting standby generators to maintain power supply in the event of mains failure is permitted only with prior approval from DEWA. The changeover circuit breaker or isolator should have four poles for three-phase supply and two poles for single-phase supply. There should be no possibility of paralleling generator supply with DEWA supply. Adequate mechanical and electrical interlock should be provided between the incomer circuit breakers or isolators of both the generator and DEWA supplies. Provision for connecting a mobile generator with incomer rating 2,500 A/1,600 A should be included in the MDB/LV panel; the circuit breaker should be a four-pole type.', a: { gen_changeover: '4-pole (3-ph); no paralleling with DEWA supply', gen_required: 'Mandatory for touristic, commercial and cultural premises (malls, hotels, hospitals…); min. 20% of landlord lighting on standby' },
  e: 'DEWA requires standby generation with ATS for malls, hotels, hospitals, museums and similar premises; essential loads include elevators, alarms, BMS, stairways and electrical/generator rooms plus at least 20% of landlord lighting. The changeover device is four-pole and must never allow paralleling with the DEWA supply.', rec: 'Submit the generator single-line diagram, essential-load list and interlock details to DEWA for approval before starting works, and make the generator room 2 h fire rated.' },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.15', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.15', cl: 'G.4.15', pg: 'G 41', f: 27, t: 'Power to fire pumps', s: ['Fire Pumps', 'Fire Alarm'], k: ['Fire Alarm', 'Electrical'], x: 'Table 9.3, Ch. 9 of UAE FLSC requires a direct power supply from the utility company to the main electric fire pump and jockey pump via a dedicated DB located inside the pump room. NOTE: It is the responsibility of the consumer to provide this arrangement at the main LV panel. For fire pumps requiring 300 A or 400 A direct feeder connections, the MDB can be replaced with the respective fire pump panel (Figure G.18).' },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.16.1', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.16', cl: 'G.4.16.1', pg: 'G 44–45', f: 28, t: 'Assessment of connected load – lighting and small power circuits', s: ['Load Estimation', 'Load Schedule', 'Socket Outlets', 'Small Power'], k: ['Electrical', 'Power', 'Low Voltage'],
  x: 'All lighting and fan circuits shall be installed as follows: a) maximum load per circuit of 2,000 W; b) minimum circuit wire/ECC size of 2.5 mm² LSF copper, with maximum circuit breaker protection of 16 A. If light fixtures are not selected at design stage, a minimum of 100 W shall be used for each normal lighting and fan point. Fluorescent lamps shall be assessed as 1.8 times the lamp wattage. A radial final sub-circuit shall be installed to serve a maximum of five 13 A switched socket outlets in rooms other than the kitchen. It shall be controlled by a 20 A circuit breaker in the DB. A maximum of ten socket outlets in rooms other than the kitchen shall be connected to a ring circuit, controlled by a 30 A circuit breaker. A current demand of 13 A shall be assumed for each 13 A switched socket outlet circuit. A minimum of 200 W per point shall be used for each 13 A switched socket outlet for general utility purpose, other than the kitchen. All twin socket outlets shall be taken to be two separate socket outlet points. For general purpose utility socket outlets, an assumed load of 1,000 W per socket outlet installed in commercial and industrial premises, and 500 W per socket outlet in residential premises, is permitted.', a: { lighting_circuit_max: '2000 W (16 A max breaker, 2.5 mm²)', ring_sockets: 'Radial: 5 × 13 A sockets on 20 A; ring: ≤ 10 sockets on 30 A', socket_load: '1000 W (commercial) / 500 W (residential) per utility socket' } },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.16.2', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.16', cl: 'G.4.16.2 / Tables G.15–G.16', pg: 'G 45, G 50', f: 30, t: 'Maximum demand and feeder/transformer limits', s: ['Load Estimation', 'Demand Factor', 'Diversity Factor', 'Transformer'], k: ['Electrical', 'Transformer'],
  x: 'All DBs shall be rated for the TCL before a demand factor is applied. An appropriate demand factor worked out by a qualified electrical engineer is permitted to determine the maximum demand at the main or sub-main distribution level. Demand factor is equal to or less than one. Table G.15 – limit of maximum demand at the MDB connected to DEWA’s supply feeder/transformer (normal residential and commercial premises without large motor loads): 60 A feeder – 30 kW; 100 A – 50 kW; 125 A – 60 kW; 160 A – 80 kW; 200 A – 100 kW; 300 A – 150 kW; 400 A – 200 kW; 1,000 kVA transformer – 800 kW; 1,500 kVA transformer – 1,200 kW. Table G.16 – DEWA transformers supplying motors and air conditioners (individual loads not exceeding 100 kW): 1,500 kVA transformer – 950 kW; 1,000 kVA transformer – 650 kW. Where individual loads exceed 100 kW, the maximum TCL shall be subject to approval by DEWA.', a: { tx_1000_md: '800 kW (650 kW with motor / A-C loads)' } },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.17', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.17', cl: 'G.4.17.1–G.4.17.3', pg: 'G 50, G 53', f: 31, t: 'Installation of conduits, flexible conduits and cable trays', s: ['Cable'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Electrical'], x: 'Draw-in boxes shall be provided in all straight conduit runs exceeding 15 m. Conduit runs having 90° bends shall be provided with draw-in boxes for every two bends. Trunking and conduit shall be completely installed before any cable is drawn in. Only flush type switches, socket outlets and accessories shall be used for concealed wiring. Non-metallic conduits shall not be used where exposed to outside ambient temperatures, where at risk of chemicals, in plant rooms, elevator motor rooms and elevator shafts. A flexible conduit run shall be not more than 2.5 m in length; flexible conduit shall not be used as part of the earth conductor. Cable ties shall not be used to support multicore cables installed on cable trays that are fitted vertically. Vertical clearance above the tray shall be not less than 1.5 times the height of the tray, or as given in the manufacturer’s recommendations.', a: { flex_conduit_max: '2.5 m' } },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.18.1', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.18', cl: 'G.4.18.1', pg: 'G 54', f: 32, t: 'Armoured cables – installation', s: ['Cable'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Electrical'], x: 'Armoured cables shall be installed directly buried in the ground, drawn through ducts, laid in concrete trenches, cleated to a wall, or mounted on cable trays. The current-carrying capacity of cables shall be determined after applying suitable correction factors based on the installation method. Cables shall be selected ensuring voltage drops within the limit described in G.4.7.3. Only armoured cables shall be used for underground installations. Typical armoured cable installation below ground (Figure G.21): armoured cable at 900 mm below ground; 150 mm impervious soil layer; 50 mm overlap of protective covers on either side; warning tape at 300 mm below ground. Where cable trays penetrate fire rated floors, walls, partitions or ceilings, the openings shall be sealed with an approved fire stopping system.', a: { cable_depth: '900 mm below ground; warning tape at 300 mm' } },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.18.2', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.18', cl: 'G.4.18.2', pg: 'G 56', f: 33, t: 'Distribution boards – location and mounting height', s: ['DB', 'MDB', 'SMDB'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Electrical'], fig: 'dbclear',
  x: 'All DBs shall be installed in locations which are accessible at all times for operation, testing, inspection, maintenance and repair. MDBs, SMDBs or final DBs shall not be installed in the following locations: a) bathrooms and toilets; b) damp or wet locations; c) bedrooms; d) kitchens; e) above sinks; f) store rooms; g) rooms with an ambient temperature exceeding the ambient design conditions of the equipment; h) dangerous or hazardous locations; i) below any staircase. DBs shall incorporate means of isolation of mains supply in the form of either a circuit breaker or an incomer isolator. Each final DB shall supply only the circuits in the floor area where the DB is located, except for applications such as staircase and common corridor lighting in high-rise buildings. In multi-consumer installations, each consumer’s DBs shall be installed within the respective consumer’s premises and shall be near to the entrance of the premises. All DBs shall be installed flush or surface mounted at a maximum height of 2 m to the top of the DB (Figure G.25). All LV panels of 1,600 A and above shall be of form 4 type.', a: { db_height: '≤ 2000 mm to top of DB' } },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.18.3', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.18', cl: 'G.4.18.3', pg: 'G 57', f: 34, t: 'Busbar trunking systems (busways / risers)', s: ['MDB'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Electrical'], x: 'Busways shall be installed only where adequate access is available for inspection and repair throughout their entire length. The design, manufacture, testing and performance of the busbar trunking system shall be in accordance with BS EN 61439. Each piece shall be subject to a 3.5 kV dielectric test for 4 s and a 1,000 V Megger test. Busbar risers in high-rise buildings shall be designed so that, in the event of outage of the respective busbar riser, not more than 12 floors shall be interrupted. The busbar trunking shall be fixed at centres of not more than 1.5 m or as recommended by the manufacturer. The busbar shall carry its rated current without exceeding 55 °C (or an ambient temperature of 50 °C at 90% relative humidity). Where the busway passes through fire rated floors, walls, partitions or ceilings, the openings shall be sealed with an approved fire stopping system. Tap-off units installed in a busbar riser at each floor level shall be at a height of 500–1,800 mm (Figure G.26).', a: { busway_tap: 'Tap-off 500–1,800 mm above floor; ≤ 12 floors per riser' } },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.18.4', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.18', cl: 'G.4.18.4–G.4.18.6', pg: 'G 58–59', f: 35, t: 'Segregation of circuits; mounting heights of accessories; labels', s: ['Socket Outlets', 'Cable', 'Lighting Controls'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Power', 'Electrical'], x: 'Circuits from different DBs shall not be installed in a common conduit or trunking. The circuit wires of individual categories (such as lighting, power and emergency) shall be segregated with barriers in trunking runs or installed in separate conduits. In kitchens and other areas where water is regularly used, switches shall not be mounted within 2 m of any water tap, wash basin or sink where possible. Socket outlets shall not be installed in bathrooms. Accessories shall be mounted as follows: a) all lighting switches, DP switches of air conditioning units and water heaters, ceiling fan regulators, shaver socket outlets, etc. shall be mounted 1.25 m above the finished floor level; b) 13 A switched socket outlets used for general purpose shall be installed at 450 mm above the finished floor level; 13 A switched socket outlets provided in kitchens shall be installed 150 mm above the worktop. All switches shall be mounted in readily accessible positions. All sections of the consumer installation at the DBs shall be provided with identification labels in both English and Arabic.', a: { switch_height: '1250 mm AFFL', socket_height: '450 mm AFFL' } },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.19.1', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.19', cl: 'G.4.19.1', pg: 'G 60–61', f: 37, t: 'Earthing and earth leakage protection – general', s: ['Earthing', 'Protection', 'Lightning Protection'], k: ['Earthing', 'Electrical'], fig: 'earthing',
  x: 'Every consumer installation shall be provided with a separate earthing system within the plot limits, installed and maintained by the consumer. ECCs shall be provided for every outgoing circuit from the MDB, SMDB and final DBs; equipotential bonding of all metalwork and exposed conductive parts and enclosures. Guidance is available in BS 7430, BS EN 50522 and IEC 60364. The consumer’s earthing system shall be connected to the DEWA earthing system. The following shall have separate earthing networks and shall not be connected to the main electrical earthing system: a) MV networks; b) LV networks; c) extra LV networks; d) private generators; e) lightning protection systems. The consumer main earthing connection shall be a TN-S system. The neutral and earth conductors shall be kept separate and shall not be connected together at the main earth terminal or at any other point in the consumer’s installation. Water mains shall not be used for earthing purposes.', a: { earth_system: 'TN-S; neutral and earth never connected in consumer installation' } },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.19.2', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.19', cl: 'G.4.19.2', pg: 'G 61–62', f: 38, t: 'Consumer’s main earth electrode', s: ['Earthing', 'Protection', 'Lightning Protection'], k: ['Earthing', 'Electrical', 'Lightning Protection'], fig: 'earthing',
  x: 'A minimum of one main earth electrode shall be provided for each incoming point of supply/consumer’s MDB, within the consumer’s premises. For installations with a main incomer of 200 A and above, a minimum of two earth pits shall be provided. The earthing systems shall consist of copper conductors or steel rods (austenitic steel or copper clad) of appropriate dimensions, set with a driving pin and head driven to a minimum depth of 3 m. The earth electrode shall be installed inside a 300 mm × 300 mm × 300 mm earth pit with inspection cover. The consumer’s main earth electrode shall be installed within 1.5 m of the MDB. Where more than one earth electrode is installed within the premises, they shall be spaced not less than 6 m apart. Load centres located laterally 50 m or more from the MDBs might require additional backup earthing. For each incoming DEWA supply/MDB, the main earth electrode resistance shall not exceed 1 Ω. The resistance from any point of the earth continuity conductor (ECC) to the main earth electrode shall not exceed 0.5 Ω. Lightning protection earthing shall be separate from the earthing of the incoming DEWA supply/MDBs. A minimum distance of 7 m shall be maintained between the earthing inspection pits.',
  a: { earth_res: '≤ 1 Ω', earth_rod_depth: '3 m', earth_pit: '300 × 300 × 300 mm', multi_electrode: '≥ 2 pits for incomers ≥ 200 A', earth_spacing: '≥ 6 m between electrodes' },
  e: 'The main earth electrode resistance must not exceed 1 Ω per incoming DEWA supply/MDB (0.5 Ω from any point of the ECC to the electrode). Use at least two earth pits for incomers of 200 A and above, rods driven to at least 3 m in 300 mm pits, spaced at least 6 m apart and within 1.5 m of the MDB. Lightning-protection earthing is separate, with 7 m between earth inspection pits.', rec: 'Show the earth pits, 6 m / 7 m spacing and the 1 Ω test requirement on the earthing drawing; keep resistance test records for inspection.' },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.19.3', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.19', cl: 'G.4.19.3 / Table G.20', pg: 'G 62', f: 39, t: 'Earth continuity conductor (ECC) sizing', s: ['Earthing', 'Cable Sizing', 'Protection'], k: ['Earthing', 'Electrical', 'Low Voltage'], x: 'Every circuit in the MDBs, SMDBs and final DBs shall be provided with a separate green and yellow (G/Y) LSF insulated copper ECC. Minimum size (Table G.20): phase/neutral S ≤ 16 mm² – ECC = S; 16 < S < 35 mm² – ECC = 16 mm²; S > 35 mm² – ECC = S/2. Equipotential bonding conductors: S ≤ 16 – S/2 (not less than 6 mm²); 16 < S < 35 – 10 mm²; S > 35 – S/4 (need not exceed 25 mm²). ECCs shall be terminated with tinned copper lugs at both ends, on purpose-made earth terminals. Gas pipes, oil pipes, metallic conduit, support wires or other flexible metallic parts shall not be used as an ECC. ECCs shall be covered with green and yellow LFC insulation. Where associated with circuits, ECCs shall be labelled at their termination points with circuit identification numbers. Circuit ECCs shall run alongside the associated phase and neutral conductor.', a: { pe_size: 'S ≤ 16: S; 16–35: 16 mm²; > 35: S/2' } },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.19.4', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.19', cl: 'G.4.19.4 / Table G.21', pg: 'G 63', f: 40, t: 'Earth leakage protection – RCD/ELCB operating currents', s: ['Protection', 'RCD', 'Socket Outlets', 'Lighting'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Electrical', 'Power'],
  x: 'Earth leakage protection shall be designed and incorporated in consumer installations in accordance with BS EN 61140 and IEC 61140. The ELCBs/RCCBs shall generally conform to BS EN 61008-1 and BS EN 61009-1. Table G.21 – recommended rated operating current (mA): 13 A switched socket outlets 30; water heater/coolers/dishwashers 30; refrigerator/washing machine and similar apparatus 30; domestic water pumps 30; Jacuzzi 10; underwater lighting 10; 15 A switched socket outlets (general purpose) 30; general lighting 30/100; flood lighting 100/300; window/split type air conditioner 100; fan coil/AHU/VAV 100; package type air-conditioning unit 100/300; chiller 100/500/1,000; irrigation pump 100; electric cooker 100; industrial machine 100/300; elevators/escalators/hoist 300/500; neon sign 300. Earth leakage sensors/relays with alarm/indicators shall be provided for fire pumps, jockey pumps, submersible pumps, sump pumps, or other essential circuit/equipment with sensor settings that allow pre-fault warning notification and fault warning notification.', a: { rcd_sockets: '30 mA', rcd_fixed: '30 / 100 mA (general lighting, Table G.21)' },
  e: 'Socket outlets, water heaters, refrigerators and similar appliances take 30 mA RCDs; general lighting 30 or 100 mA; air-conditioning and fan coil units 100 mA; chillers 100/500/1,000 mA. Essential pumps need earth-leakage sensors with alarm rather than tripping.', rec: 'Use Table G.21 to set the RCD/RCBO rating on every DB schedule line and add earth-leakage alarm relays on fire, jockey and sump pump circuits.' },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.19.5', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.19', cl: 'G.4.19.5', pg: 'G 64', f: 41, t: 'Equipotential bonding', s: ['Earthing', 'Protection'], k: ['Earthing', 'Electrical'], x: 'All metalwork of the consumer’s installation, other than current-carrying parts, shall be provided with equipotential bonding conductors. This shall include, but is not limited to: cable armour; metal conduits; metal cable tray/trunking sections; metal accessory boxes; exposed metalworks of consumers’ appliances; apparatus; equipment; machines; building structures; metallic enclosures and parts; metal water pipes. The cross-sectional area of equipotential bonding conductors shall be selected using Table G.20. A main protective bonding conductor shall have a minimum cross-sectional area not less than half the cross-sectional area required for ECC of the installation, and not less than 6 mm². A maximum cross-sectional area of 25 mm² is sufficient if the bonding conductor is of copper. The equipotential bonding conductors shall be connected to the main earthing terminal within the consumer’s wiring installations. The continuity shall be tested and maintained by the consumer.', a: { main_bonding: '≥ ½ ECC, min 6 mm², max 25 mm²' } },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.20.1', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.20', cl: 'G.4.20.1', pg: 'G 65–66', f: 42, t: 'Power factor correction', s: ['Power Factor'], k: ['Electrical', 'Low Voltage'],
  x: 'The PF of every consumer installation shall be within the range of 0.9 lagging and unity (recommended value 0.95 lagging). All air-conditioning units/plants/equipment, machines, motors, light fittings with discharge lamps/mercury vapour/sodium vapour/fluorescent tubes, etc., shall be provided with capacitors or other approved means to achieve and maintain a PF of 0.95 lagging or above, throughout their normal working range. For commercial premises that require DEWA service feeders of 200 A and above, where individual load compensation cannot be achieved, overall compensation at main or sub-main distribution levels shall be provided by incorporating capacitor banks with automatic regulated steps. For residential premises the limitation shall be a 400 A feeder. The PF correction capacitor shall be a dry, encapsulated, sealed type conforming to IEC 61921. Capacitors shall be designed for temperature class D. Harmonic filter reactors shall be provided in series with capacitors. The capacitor bank panel shall have a three-pole isolator or MCCB whose handle is interlocked with the door. Capacitor banks shall be provided with forced ventilation and double enclosures for outdoor/open to sky installations.', a: { pf: '0.9 lagging to unity (recommended 0.95)' },
  e: 'DEWA requires the power factor to stay between 0.9 lagging and unity, recommended 0.95 lagging. Equipment such as A/C, motors and discharge lighting should carry their own correction, and commercial feeders of 200 A and above (400 A residential) need an automatic capacitor bank with harmonic filter reactors.', rec: 'Specify an automatically stepped capacitor bank with detuning reactors, an interlocked isolator and forced ventilation, in a separate cubicle, and show the PF meter on the MDB.' },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.20.2', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.20', cl: 'G.4.20.2–G.4.20.3', pg: 'G 66–67', f: 43, t: 'Harmonics and undervoltage (UV) relays with auto-reset timer', s: ['Power Factor', 'HVAC', 'Protection'], k: ['Electrical', 'Power'], x: 'A consumer’s load shall not cause deviations of the voltage characteristics other than those permitted by BS EN 50160 and IEC 61000. All air-conditioners and air-conditioning units/plants/equipment installed within the consumer’s installation shall be provided with UV relays that trip the circuit breakers/contactors associated with these relays. The tripping shall occur immediately and without any intentional time delay if the supply voltage drops to or below 75% of nominal and remains at or below 75% for a duration of 0.2 s. The UV relay’s auto-reset timer shall have an adjustable time setting between 5 min and 10 min. The circuit breakers/contactors associated with the UV relay shall have an auto-closing facility (motorized operation) to restore supply to the chillers/air conditioning units. The UV relays shall not be applied to trip LV incomer circuit-breakers/ACB within the main LV panel.', a: { uv_relay: '75% of nominal, 0.2 s; auto-reset 5–10 min' } },
{ id: 'dbc-G.4.21', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.4.21', cl: 'G.4.21.1–G.4.21.3', pg: 'G 67–68', f: 44, t: 'Construction sites – supply, wiring and earth leakage protection', s: ['Protection', 'RCD', 'Socket Outlets'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Power'], x: 'Assemblies for the distribution of electricity on construction sites shall conform to BS 4363 and BS EN 61439. Cables shall not be installed across a site, road or walkway unless the cable is protected against mechanical damage. All cables used on construction sites shall have a metal sheath and/or armour, both of which shall be effectively earthed and continuous. Metering cabinets, DBs and wiring installations installed outdoors shall be weatherproof (IP 65 in accordance with BS EN 60529). A means of emergency switching shall be provided on the supply to all equipment from which it might be necessary to disconnect all live conductors in order to remove a hazard. In addition to the overcurrent and short-circuit protection, every circuit shall be protected for earth leakage. All of the following shall be protected with an ELCB/RCCB of rated operating current 30 mA: a) final sub-circuits connected to 13 A switched socket outlets; b) portable tools; c) equipment. A 100 mA ELCB/RCCB shall be provided for the protection of other lighting circuits, fixed equipment, etc. unless otherwise specified.', a: { rcd_construction: '30 mA socket outlets/portable tools; 100 mA other lighting & fixed equipment' } },
{ id: 'dbc-G.5.1', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.5.1', cl: 'G.5.1.1–G.5.1.5', pg: 'G 69–70', f: 46, t: 'Electric vehicle charging modes', s: ['Protection'], k: ['Electrical', 'Power'], x: 'IEC 61851 specifies four different modes of conductive charging for electric vehicles. Mode 1 charging (AC via a standard socket outlet and a charging cable without communication function) is not permitted due to the lack of safety measures associated with this mode of charging. Mode 2 charging: the charging cable assembly incorporates an in-cable control box; the final circuit, protective device and socket shall be of a suitable rating for the connected load, and shall not exceed 32 A. Mode 3 charging employs dedicated EV service equipment (EVSE) and a charging cable assembly with a control pilot cable. Mode 4 charging employs an off-board charger to deliver direct current to the battery (20 kW to 120 kW). NOTE: Maximum charging capacity of a DEWA charger shall be limited to 25 kW or below per charging outlet, subject to available load capacity.', a: { ev_modes: 'Mode 1 not permitted; Mode 2 ≤ 32 A; DEWA chargers ≤ 25 kW per outlet' } },
{ id: 'dbc-G.5.2', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.5.2', cl: 'G.5.2', pg: 'G 71', f: 48, t: 'EVSE design requirements', s: ['Socket Outlets', 'Protection', 'Cable Sizing'], k: ['Electrical', 'Power', 'Low Voltage'], x: 'Installation of a private charging outlet shall be subject to prior DEWA approval. EVSE shall not be installed in locations where a potentially explosive atmosphere exists, such as petrol stations. EVSE shall be supplied by a separate and dedicated radial circuit. Cables supplying EVSE shall be mechanically protected by means of metal sheath/armour, or installed inside a conduit made from rigid steel, plastic or PVC. Each final circuit shall be sized to carry the rated current of the EVSE, with limited voltage drop. There shall be no diversity on a final circuit supplying more than one EVSE. One socket outlet and/or vehicle connector shall be used to charge one EV. The main operating controls and any socket outlet shall be between 0.75 m and 1.2 m above ground. A means of isolating the supply to the EVSE circuit shall be provided, capable of being locked in the open position. Each final circuit shall be individually protected against fault current by a suitably rated overcurrent protective device.', a: { evse_height: '0.75–1.2 m above ground (controls and socket)' } },
{ id: 'dbc-G.6', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.6', cl: 'G.6.1–G.6.5', pg: 'G 73–74', f: 49, t: 'Renewable energy – solar PV connection, documentation and labelling', s: ['Protection', 'Earthing'], k: ['Electrical', 'Lightning Protection'], x: 'The onsite generation of electricity from a solar PV shall be by a solar grid-connected system, connected to the DEWA network. Solar power generation systems shall conform to Section 2, Ch. 14 of the UAE FLSC and this section. Building attached photovoltaics (BAPV) systems attached to roofs are permitted to achieve a minimum fire classification of Class C when tested as per the test standards in Section 2.2.4, Ch. 14 of the UAE FLSC. Applications for solar connections shall be submitted online and shall include basic system information, details of the system designer, and details of the system installer, operation and maintenance procedure. An annotated single line wiring diagram shall be uploaded with the DEWA application, including PV array specifications, string cable and fuse specifications, DC isolator details, earthing and overvoltage protection, and AC isolator, overcurrent and residual current device details. Labelling: all DC junction boxes shall be provided with caution labels indicating the risk due to dual source; the main AC isolating switch shall be clearly labelled; a single line wiring diagram and the procedure for emergency shutdown shall be displayed within the respective electrical rooms/panels. DEWA requires access to meter cabinets for the installation of smart meters.' },
{ id: 'dbc-G.7.1', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.7.1', cl: 'G.7.1.1–G.7.1.7', pg: 'G 75–76', f: 51, t: 'MV network design requirements (11 kV)', s: ['MV Switchgear', 'Transformer', 'Short Circuit'], k: ['Medium Voltage', 'Transformer'], x: 'A ring supply consisting of two feeders (a two-feed ring) is the normal feeding arrangement for power supply. For reliable power supply, an N-1 line criterion may be used; in case of power failure in one feeder, the other feeder should be capable of meeting the demand for a maximum of 6 h. The DEWA standard 11 kV cable sizes are: 3/C 300 mm² copper XLPE; 3/C 240 mm² copper XLPE; 3/C 240 mm² aluminium XLPE. The maximum sustained load of an 11 kV feeder is 175 A/3 MW for 300 mm² copper XLPE cables (summer rating) and 160 A/2.7 MW for 240 mm² copper XLPE. All 11 kV private switchgear shall have a rated symmetrical short-circuit current not less than 31.5 kA, with a short time current rating of 3 s. Parallel operation of DEWA’s MV feeders is not allowed. Standby generators are not allowed to operate in parallel with DEWA’s network. The customer shall maintain a PF between 0.95 (lagging) and unity at the point of connection with DEWA’s MV network. Maximum starting current: motors ≤ 600 kW – 6 × full load current; 600–1,200 kW – 4×; 1,200–1,800 kW – 3×; 1,800–2,400 kW – 2×; 2,400–3,000 kW – 1.5×; maximum current per feeder during motor starting should not exceed 350 A. Cable trenches: maximum 20 11 kV cables in two layers; ≥ 2 m clearance from heat sources such as 132 kV cable trench; horizontal spacing 150 mm and vertical spacing 100 mm (edge to edge).', a: { mv_feeder_max: '175 A / 3 MW (300 mm² Cu XLPE)', mv_fault_level: '31.5 kA / 3 s private MV switchgear' } },
{ id: 'dbc-G.7.2', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.7.2', cl: 'G.7.2.1–G.7.2.3', pg: 'G 77', f: 53, t: 'Substation construction within private plots – location and parameters', s: ['Transformer', 'RMU'], k: ['Transformer', 'Medium Voltage'], fig: 'transroom',
  x: 'Pocket (self-contained) substations shall not be installed at petrol stations and inside buildings. Expansion joints shall not be used in RMU rooms or transformer rooms, or on ceilings. The substation shall be positioned in a dedicated room or housing. Substations located in basement levels shall have transformer rooms in the first basement level only. LV electrical rooms shall be adjacent to the substation room if the main panel is private. Wet areas shall not be installed above substations. A single room substation at ground floor shall have a clear height of not less than 3,700 mm. For split/basement substation arrangements, the RMU room at ground floor shall have a clear height of not less than 3 m. A basement transformer room shall have a clear height of not less than 3 m. The substation finished floor level (FFL) shall be maintained between 150 mm and 300 mm above the adjacent ground level (towards the door side). The FFL of a transformer room at basement level shall be maintained between 75 mm and 150 mm higher than the adjacent outside ground level (towards door side).', a: { tx_room_height: '3.7 m (single room ground floor); 3.0 m (RMU / basement transformer room)', tx_ffl: '+0.15 to +0.30 m above adjacent ground (ground floor)' } },
{ id: 'dbc-G.7.2.4', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.7.2', cl: 'G.7.2.4', pg: 'G 80', f: 54, t: 'Substation earthing', s: ['Earthing', 'Transformer'], k: ['Earthing', 'Transformer', 'Medium Voltage'], x: 'Substation earthing, which forms part of the consumer’s scope, shall meet DEWA requirements at design stage. a) Two or more dedicated and interconnected earth pits shall be provided for transformer earthing, connected to the substation earth strips in a loop. The effective earth resistance value measured from any neutral earthing strip shall be less than 2.0 Ω. b) Two or more dedicated and interconnected earth pits shall be provided for transformer equipment body earthing; the effective earth resistance value measured from any of the equipment body earth strips shall be less than 2.0 Ω. c) Interconnection of transformer neutral earthing and equipment body earthing is not permitted. d) Earth strips shall be mounted on the cable trench wall with a minimum clearance of 50 mm from the wall. e) Earth strips shall be provided with a minimum of four 12 mm diameter holes for earth looping. f) Earth conductors shall be copper with a minimum of 95 mm²; if the length of conductors exceeds 50 m, 120 mm² copper earth conductors shall be used.', a: { sub_earth: '< 2.0 Ω (neutral and body earth separate); 95 mm² Cu (120 mm² if > 50 m)' } },
{ id: 'dbc-G.7.3', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.7.3–G.7.4', cl: 'G.7.3–G.7.4', pg: 'G 81', f: 55, t: 'Substation location, access and attic slab above substations', s: ['Transformer', 'RMU'], k: ['Transformer', 'Medium Voltage'], x: 'The substation/RMU room shall be directly located on the RTA/public road or sikka. Where the substation/RMU room is located on a sikka, the sikka should have a minimum clear width of 6.1 m. However, if the proposed RMU room location is less than 12 m away from the main road then the sikka can be used with a minimum clear width of 3 m. Access shall be provided for DEWA staff and vehicles from the plot limits to the substation and RMU rooms; the access shall be directly open to sky and available 24/7. In a split room/basement room arrangement, the transformer room may be located on an internal driveway having a clear 3 m wide × 3 m high and direct access from the RTA road. Wet facilities shall not normally be located above the substation/RMU room/LV electrical room; if unavoidable, a reinforced concrete attic slab shall be provided with waterproofing, a void of not less than 600 mm clear height accessed via a 1,200 mm × 600 mm aluminium louvered opening, a drain pipe, and lighting inside the void.' },
{ id: 'dbc-G.7.5.1', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.7.5', cl: 'G.7.5.1 / Table G.23', pg: 'G 82', f: 56, t: 'Single room substation – dimensions', s: ['Transformer', 'RMU'], k: ['Transformer', 'Medium Voltage'], x: 'The dimensions for a single room substation where the RMU and transformer are in the same room on the ground floor shall conform to Table G.23: 1 × 1,000/1,500 kVA – area required 33 m², minimum width of room 4.57 m; 2 × 1,000/1,500 kVA – 55 m², 6.1 m; additional transformer – additional 25 m²; four or more transformers (including necessary equipment) – 10 m². Figure G.39 (typical arrangement, for reference only): louvered windows 3.0 m (w) × 2.15 m (h), installed 0.6 m AFFL; 150 mm ø PVC at trench level.', a: { tx_room_size: '33 m², min. width 4.57 m (single room, 1×1,000/1,500 kVA)' } },
{ id: 'dbc-G.7.5.2', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.7.5', cl: 'G.7.5.2–G.7.5.3', pg: 'G 85, G 87', f: 57, t: 'Split room and basement substation – RMU and transformer rooms', s: ['Transformer', 'RMU'], k: ['Transformer', 'Medium Voltage'], x: 'Split room substation (RMU room, ground floor – Table G.24): one RMU set controlling two transformers – 9 m², minimum width 3 m towards door side; additional RMU set – 7 m²; four or more transformers (including necessary equipment) – 10 m², minimum width 5 m. Basement substation (Tables G.26–G.27): RMU room as above; transformer room (basement level) 1 × 1,000/1,500 kVA – 21 m², minimum width 4.57 m; extra area required for an additional transformer – 21 m²; 2 × 1,000/1,500 kVA – 42 m², 6.1 m.' },
{ id: 'dbc-G.7.5.4', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.7.5', cl: 'G.7.5.4–G.7.5.6', pg: 'G 88–91', f: 59, t: 'Open-to-sky, pocket substations and DEWA control room for direct 11 kV supply', s: ['Transformer', 'RMU', 'MV Switchgear'], k: ['Transformer', 'Medium Voltage'], x: 'Open to sky dedicated substation with private panel (Table G.28): 1 × 1,000/1,500 kVA transformer and 1 × RMU; dimensions 6.1 m × 6.1 m; substation space shall be located along RTA/public road or sikka; LV room shall be adjacent to the substation room; soakaway shall be 3.66 m (minimum) away from the substation; compound wall not more than 2.1 m. Open to sky pocket substation (Table G.29): 1 × 1,000 kVA – 4.57 × 3.66 m; 2 × 1,000 kVA – 6.1 × 6.1 m; suitable for releasing supply through individual feeders, each of 400 A (maximum rating); wall height around the foundation (three sides) not more than 2,100 mm; any soakaway a minimum of 3.66 m from the substation. DEWA control room for direct 11 kV supply (Table G.30): two feeders – 29.16 m² (5.4 × 5.4 m), regular RMU room of block work and RCC slab, 8 m² for every additional feeder; or open to sky 37.2 m² (6.1 × 6.1 m) with FGRP kiosk supplied by DEWA.' },
{ id: 'dbc-G.7.6', d: 'dbc21', ch: 'G', sec: 'G.7.6', cl: 'G.7.6.1–G.7.6.3', pg: 'G 93', f: 62, t: 'Substation ventilation and fire safety provisions', s: ['Transformer', 'RMU', 'Generator'], k: ['Transformer', 'Medium Voltage'], x: 'Substations shall have adequate ventilation using natural louvers, mechanical ventilation or a combination of both. Substations shall be 2 h fire rated to conform to Table 1.9, Ch. 1 of UAE FLSC. Substations at ground floor shall be naturally ventilated; a minimum of two sides shall be ventilated using aluminium louvered doors and fixed aluminium louvers. Fixed aluminium louver window(s) should be at 600 mm above the outside ground level. Aluminium louvered doors shall be: transformer rooms 3.05 m wide × 2.75 m high; RMU rooms 2.4 m or 3.05 m wide × 2.75 m high. Independent mechanical ventilation shall be provided to transformer rooms with no exterior walls (e.g. in a basement). The mechanical ventilation shall be sized to maintain the ambient temperature in the substation at a maximum of 55 °C based on an outside air temperature of 48 °C; supporting calculations shall be provided. Louvers shall be provided on two sides of the room as a back-up to the mechanical ventilation system. The total grill area shall be not less than 14.9 m² for a 1,000 kVA transformer and not less than 18.6 m² for a 1,500 kVA transformer. Louvers shall be protected by automatic 2 h fire rated shutters or 2 h fire rated curtains on the landlord side of the louvers. Two combined fire and smoke detectors shall be provided on the landlord side of the louvers.', a: { tx_vent_grill: '≥ 14.9 m² (1,000 kVA) / 18.6 m² (1,500 kVA); room max 55 °C at 48 °C outside' } }
);
